    "rationale": "Chose NO_FIX. The scope I was given (uart_rx.v:170-180) contains only the cycle_counter register - the path's startpoint flop - and none of the 12 combinational cells that make up 0.86 of the 0.94 ns. RETIME rejected: there is no register inside the combinational chain to move; the only flop in scope is the launching one, and its own increment adder is not on the path (the path exits Q into a downstream decode), so pulling the register back or pushing it forward cannot split the 0.62 ns NOR4/NAND4/OAI22/AOI211/AND3/AND3/NAND3 decode. RESTRUCTURE rejected: the only in-scope combinational logic is cycle_counter + 1'b1 and the three-way fsm_state compare, neither of which appears on the reported path, so any rewrite would be a token edit with zero slack impact. PIPELINE rejected: cycle_counter feeds back into itself (increment term and the next_bit clear term), so a register inserted in this block is a cut inside a single-register feedback loop - it would alter the sampled bit period, i.e. change function, not timing. The natural cut point is next_bit / the write-enable term before it reaches the FIFO, but that signal is assigned outside my line range, and its reconvergent partners (FIFO write data and the wptr_full occupancy compare at wptr_full.v:52) would also need re-balancing outside this scope. The proper fix is a registered terminal-count flag: evaluate cycle_counter == bit_duration-1 one cycle early and flop it, so the flop drives the write-enable AND-tree directly rather than a five-level equality decode - latency-neutral, but it must be written where next_bit is generated.",
    "out_of_scope_fix": {
      "src_file": "rtl/vendor/uart/rtl/uart_rx.v",
      "start_line": 150,
      "end_line": 169,
      "confidence": "low",
      "reason": "The dominant 0.62 ns of the path is the combinational equality/threshold decode of cycle_counter qualified by fsm_state that produces next_bit (NOR4-NAND4-OAI22-NOR2-AOI211-AND3-AND3-NAND3), which is assigned outside the cycle_counter always block I was permitted to edit; converting it into a registered terminal-count flag there fixes the path with no latency change."
